CORS stands for Cross-Origin Resource Sharing. It’s a security feature used by web browsers to control how resources (like APIs, images, or scripts) are shared between different websites (origins).


1. Victim logs into siteA.com
   ↓
2. Browser stores siteA.com cookies
   ↓
3. Victim visits evil.com
   ↓
4. evil.com JavaScript sends request TO siteA.com
   ↓
5. Browser automatically includes siteA.com cookies
   because request destination is siteA.com



#Malicous Rule
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * = Any website/domain is allowed to access this resource
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true = Browser is allowed to send credentials with the request

